s="\"Proceedings of the 2015 Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA) | SIAM Publications Library (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/book/10.1137/1.9781611973730)\\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; A recent result of Ene et al. says that, under the mild technical condition that Γ contains the equality relation, the basic LP relaxation is optimal for constant-factor approximation for Min CSP(Γ) unless the Unique Games Conjecture fails. ... In this paper, we leverage insights from optimization theory to construct a novel algorithm that breaks the longstanding Õ(ε^{−4}) barrier. ... ### Robust randomized matchings ... For this setting, we devise a simple algorithm that returns a 1/ln(4)-robust randomized matching. ... We also show that our robustness results for randomized matchings translate to an asymptotic robustness guarantee for deterministic matchings: When restricting Bob's choice to cardinalities larger than a given constant, then Alice can find a single deterministic matching with approximately the same guaranteed payoff as in the randomized setting.\\n### Approximately Stable, School Optimal, and Student-Truthful Many-to-One Matchings (via Differential Privacy)\\n\\n  * Sampath Kannan, \\n  * Jamie Morgenstern, \\n  * Aaron Roth, \\n  * Zhiwei Steven Wu\\n\\npp. 1890–1903\\n\\n  * Abstract\\n  * PDF\\n\\nAbstract\\n\\nWe present a mechanism for computing asymptotically stable school optimal matchings, while guaranteeing that it is an asymptotic dominant strategy for every student to report their true preferences to the mechanism. Our main tool in this endeavor is differential privacy: we give an algorithm that coordinates a stable matching using differentially private signals, which lead to our truthfulness guarantee. This is the first setting in which it is known how to achieve nontrivial truthfulness guarantees for students when computing school optimal matchings, assuming worst-case preferences (for schools and students) in large markets.\\n\\n### Robust randomized matchings\\n\\n  * Jannik Matuschke, \\n  * Martin Skutella, \\n  * José A. Soto\\n\\npp. 1904–1915\\n\\n  * Abstract\\n  * PDF\\n\\nAbstract\\n\\nThe following zero-sum game is played on a weighted graph G: Alice selects a matching M in G and Bob selects a number k. Then, Alice receives a payoff equal to the ratio of the weight of the top k edges of M to opt_{k}, which is the maximum weight of a matching of size at most k in G. If M guarantees a payoff of at least α then it is called α-robust. In 2002, Hassin and Rubinstein gave an algorithm that returns a -robust matching, which is best possible for this setting.\\n\\nIn this paper, we show that Alice can improve on the guarantee of when allowing her to play a randomized strategy. For this setting, we devise a simple algorithm that returns a 1/ln(4)-robust randomized matching. The algorithm is based on the following non-trivial observation: If all edge weights are integer powers of 2, then any lexicographically optimum matching is 1-robust. We prove this property not only for matchings but for any independence system in which opt_{k} is a concave function of k. This class of systems includes matroid intersection, b-matchings, and strong 2-exchange systems. We also show that our robustness results for randomized matchings translate to an asymptotic robustness guarantee for deterministic matchings: When restricting Bob's choice to cardinalities larger than a given constant, then Alice can find a single deterministic matching with approximately the same guaranteed payoff as in the randomized setting. In addition to the above results, we also give a new simple LP-based proof of Hassin and Rubinstein's original result.\\n\\n### The size of the core in assignment markets\\n\\n  * Yash Kanoria, \\n  * Daniela Saban, \\n  * Jay Sethuraman\\n\\npp. 1916–1924\\n\\n  * Abstract\\n  * PDF\\n\\nAbstract\\n\\nAssignment markets involve matching with transfers, as in labor markets and housing markets. We consider a two-sided assignment market with agent types and stochastic structure similar to models used in empirical studies, and characterize the size of the core in such markets. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDiscrepancies of subtrees — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/discrepancies-of-subtrees)\\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; As our main result, we show that the $r$-colour discrepancy of the subtrees of any tree is a linear function of the number of leaves $\\\\ell$ of that tree. ... While classical results often focus on discrepancies of paths or spanning trees in structured graphs like grids, this research investigates the set $\\\\mathcal{T}$ of all subtrees within a base tree $T$.\\n\\nThe central question is: if the edges of $T$ are colored with $r$ colors, how large must the imbalance—the difference between the most and least frequent colors—be in the most \\\"unbalanced\\\" subtree? ... Additionally, they propose a conjecture for \\\"complex discrepancy\\\" (colors on the unit circle), suggesting a bound of $\\\\ell/\\\\pi$.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nThe research establishes several tight bounds:\\n\\n  * Multi-color Discrepancy: For a tree with $\\\\ell$ leaves and $r$ colors, the discrepancy $\\\\mathcal{D}_r(T, \\\\mathcal{T})$ satisfies: $$\\\\lceil (r-1)\\\\ell/r \\\\rceil \\\\le \\\\mathcal{D}_r(T, \\\\mathcal{T}) \\\\le \\\\lceil (r-1)\\\\ell/2 \\\\rceil$$ For $r=2$, the discrepancy is exactly $\\\\lceil \\\\ell/2 \\\\rceil$.\\n\\n  * Oriented Discrepancy: For directed rooted trees, the discrepancy is bounded between $\\\\lceil \\\\ell/2 \\\\rceil + 1$ and $\\\\ell$.\\n\\n  * High-Dimensional Bound: The $d$-dimensional discrepancy is bounded below by a function of the leaves and the Beta function: $\\\\mathcal{D}^d(T, \\\\mathcal{T}) \\\\ge \\\\frac{\\\\ell}{d \\\\cdot B(d/2, 1/2)}$.\\n\\nThese results also lead to improved lower bounds for discrepancy in $m \\\\times n$ grids, specifically $\\\\frac{r-1}{4r}mn + 1 - 2r$.\\n\\n## Conclusion and Future Directions\\n\\nWhile this work provides sharp bounds for multi-color discrepancy, gaps remain in the oriented and high-dimensional cases. The authors conjecture that the oriented discrepancy is closer to $\\\\ell/2$ than $\\\\ell$. Additionally, they propose a conjecture for \\\"complex discrepancy\\\" (colors on the unit circle), suggesting a bound of $\\\\ell/\\\\pi$. Future research may also focus on finding matching upper bounds for the high-dimensional geometric discrepancy to determine its exact sharpness.\\n\\nBibTeX\\n    \\n    @misc{krishna2023discrepancies,\\n      author = {Tarun Krishna and Peleg Michaeli and Michail Sarantis and Fenglin Wang and Yiqing Wang},\\n      title = {{Discrepancies of subtrees}},\\n      year = {2023},\\n      eprint = {2302.08557},\\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08557},\\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nDatabase | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/database?sort=newest&tag=discrepancy)\\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * ## Erdős Problem #994 — Khintchine's measurable-set equidistribution conjecture ...   * ## Complex discrepancy conjecture for trees ... Let $T$T be a tree with $\\\\ell$ℓ leaves, let $\\\\mathcal{T}$T denote the family of trees used in the discrepancy definition, and let $\\\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\\\mathcal{T})$D 1(T,T) denote the one-dimensi…\\n0 0 7\\n\\n  * ## Verstraete's positive discrepancy conjecture for moderately dense graphs\\n\\nAbout 3 years old\\n\\nVerstraete's conjecture.\\n\\nopendiscrepancygraph-theorymax-cut\\n\\n0 0 13\\n\\n  * ## Complex discrepancy conjecture for trees\\n\\nAbout 3 years old\\n\\nLet $T$T be a tree with $\\\\ell$ℓ leaves, let $\\\\mathcal{T}$T denote the family of trees used in the discrepancy definition, and let $\\\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\\\mathcal{T})$D 1(T,T) denote the one-dimensi…\\n\\nopendiscrepancygraph-theorytrees\\n\\n0 0 11\\n\\n  * ## Asymptotic oriented discrepancy conjecture for directed rooted trees\\n\\nAbout 3 years old\\n\\nLet $T$T be a tree with $\\\\ell$ℓ leaves, and let $\\\\mathcal{DT}$D T be the set of all directed rooted trees, namely trees with a distinguished root and all edges oriented away from it. Wr…\\n\\nopendiscrepancygraph-theorytrees\\n\\n0 0 11\\n\\n  * ## The XOP2 characterization of binary disc2,3-error\\n\\nAt least 4 years old\\n\\nLet $\\\\mathcal{L}={R(x,y,z)}$L=R(x,y,z) be the language of ternary $3$3-graph relations, and let $\\\\text{XOP}_2$XOP 2​ be a proposed definition given by an infinite scheme of existential sentences. X…\\n\\nopendiscrepancyhypergraph-regularitymodel-theory\\n\\n0 0 7\\n\\n  * ## The hereditary-discrepancy characterization of nowhere dense classes\\n\\nAbout 5 years old\\n\\nHereditary-discrepancy characterization. A monotone class $\\\\mathscr{C}$C is nowhere dense if and only if, for every partitioned formula $\\\\varphi(\\\\bar{x};\\\\bar{y})$φ(x ˉ;y ˉ​), every…\\n\\nopendiscrepancyfinite-model-theorynowhere-dense-classes\\n\\n0 0 9\\n\\n  * ## The computational hardness conjecture for constant discrepancy\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-09-16&page=52&range=3)\\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; ~Kobzar}, title = {{T}he {K}omlós conjecture for complex discrepancy}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.15071}, note = {arXiv:2609.15071v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The Komlós conjecture is a classic problem in discrepancy theory; it asks whether an absolute constant $K$ exists such that given any $n$ vectors $a_1,\\\\ldots,a_n$ inside the $m$-dimensional Euclidean ball, regardless of how large $m,n$ are, there is always a selection of signs $\\\\varepsilon_1,\\\\ldots,\\\\varepsilon_n$ guaranteeing $$\\\\|\\\\varepsilon_1a_1+\\\\ldots+\\\\varepsilon_na_n\\\\|_∞≤K.\\nNestor Guillen, Vladimir A. Kobzar\\n\\nSep 15 2026 math.CO cs.DM math.CA math.CV arXiv:2609.15071v2\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2609.15071, author = {Nestor Guillen and Vladimir A.~Kobzar}, title = {{T}he {K}omlós conjecture for complex discrepancy}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.15071}, note = {arXiv:2609.15071v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nThe Komlós conjecture is a classic problem in discrepancy theory; it asks whether an absolute constant $K$ exists such that given any $n$ vectors $a_1,\\\\ldots,a_n$ inside the $m$-dimensional Euclidean ball, regardless of how large $m,n$ are, there is always a selection of signs $\\\\varepsilon_1,\\\\ldots,\\\\varepsilon_n$ guaranteeing $$\\\\|\\\\varepsilon_1a_1+\\\\ldots+\\\\varepsilon_na_n\\\\|_∞≤K.$$ We show that if the $\\\\varepsilon_i$'s are allowed to take not just the values of $\\\\pm 1$ but any unit modulus complex number, which we refer to as complex discrepancy, then the above inequality holds for a finite, explicit constant $K_{\\\\mathbb{C}}$. Here, the $\\\\ell^\\\\infty$ norm of the resulting vector in $\\\\mathbb{C}^m$ is the largest modulus of its entries, and thus the complex discrepancy of real vectors is equivalent to their rank-$2$ vector discrepancy. This quantity provides an upper bound (modulo uniform constant prefactor) on Gaussian discrepancy -- a discrepancy measure introduced by Chewi, Gerber, Rigollet and Turner. Thus, we also resolve the Komlós conjecture for Gaussian discrepancy. Our paper builds upon the recent work of Bansal and Jiang on the Beck-Fiala and Komlós conjectures, which we approach from the formalism of Burkholder and the Bellman function method from probability and harmonic analysis. Our work was in part motivated by the realization that the complex discrepancy of the columns of any unitary matrix is equal to 1, a fact that follows from a straightforward calculation based on Idel and Wolf's generalization of the Sinkhorn normal form for unitary matrices.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCombinatorics: AI research tracker · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/problems/combinatorics)\\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; ### A counterexample to Ryser's covering conjecture ... Read paper Reported 23 Sept 2026 ... For every sufficiently large prime $s\\\\equiv 2 \\\\mod  3$s≡2 mod 3 and sufficiently large odd n depending on s, with $r=s^{n}+1$r=s n+1, the manuscript constructs an r-edge-colored complete graph requiring exactly r monochromatic trees to cover its vertices, even when trees may overlap and singletons count.\\n### A counterexample to Ryser's covering conjecture\\n\\nOpenAI\\n\\nFor every sufficiently large prime $s\\\\equiv 2 \\\\mod  3$s≡2 mod 3 and sufficiently large odd n depending on s, the September 23 manuscript constructs an intersecting $(s^{n}+1)$(s n+1)-partite uniform hypergraph with matching number one and covering number $s^{n}+1$s n+1, exceeding Ryser’s proposed bound $s^{n}$s n.\\n\\nAI involvementAI-led\\n\\nImportanceMajor\\n\\nTechnical verificationFormal (Lean 4 with Mathlib and Comparator target)\\n\\nHuman understandingFew\\n\\nRead paperReported 23 Sept 2026\\n\\n### A counterexample to Ryser's covering conjecture\\n\\nOpenAI\\n\\nFor every sufficiently large prime $s\\\\equiv 2 \\\\mod  3$s≡2 mod 3 and sufficiently large odd n depending on s, with $r=s^{n}+1$r=s n+1, the manuscript constructs an r-edge-colored complete graph requiring exactly r monochromatic trees to cover its vertices, even when trees may overlap and singletons count.\\n\\nAI involvementAI-led\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConjectures | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/conjectures)\\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Discrete structures Question resolved First preprint 2026 Turning increasing trees into permutations—and back We construct the forward and inverse maps for the entire stated tree family and prove both inverse identities for every positive parameter pair.Testart Question 1: the native tree side and a type-C permutation model Proved ... Scope & next question The result answers the tree-side question. ... Proof explanation Original question Release problem dossier ...   * Historical context ... An explicitly identified research question in the original source. ... The delivered argument gives a structural characterization or normal form.\\n\\nDiscrete structures Question resolved First preprint 2026 Turning increasing trees into permutations—and back We construct the forward and inverse maps for the entire stated tree family and prove both inverse identities for every positive parameter pair.Testart Question 1: the native tree side and a type-C permutation model Proved\\n\\nThe question Can Testart’s increasing trees be matched explicitly to another family counted by type-C poly-Bernoulli numbers?\\n\\nWhy it matters The source asks for a bijective explanation. The target permutation family has a prescribed set of strict excedance positions.\\n\\nThe idea Read each tree component into a directed cycle. In reverse, recover every parent from the rightmost preceding smaller label.\\n\\nOur contribution We construct the forward and inverse maps for the entire stated tree family and prove both inverse identities for every positive parameter pair.\\n\\nScope & next question The result answers the tree-side question. The published count of the permutation family remains an attributed literature result.\\n\\nVerification details\\n\\nRelease source record Proved in Lean\\n\\narXiv\\n\\n### Testart Question 1: the native tree side and a type-C permutation model\\n\\nBenjamin Testart, On minimal pattern-containing inversion sequences, arXiv:2602.12130v2, Section 7, Question 1, printed page 17, asks:\\n\\n> Find bijections between the families $\\\\mathrm{IP}{n,k}$ or $\\\\mathcal T{n,k}$ of Section 6 and other families of combinatorial objects known to be counted by the poly-Bernoulli numbers of type C.\\n\\nThe question offers an IP-side or a T-side answer. The target here is the entire T-side family, rather than the source's already published IP/T correspondence. Section 6 defines unordered rooted increasing trees. For every pair of integers $a,b\\\\ge1$, write $N=a+b-1$. The source $T(a,b)$ consists of trees on distinct labels $0,\\\\ldots,N$, rooted at zero, whose internal labels are exactly $0,\\\\ldots,a-1$ and whose leaves are exactly $a,\\\\ldots,N$. Every parent label is smaller than its child label. The target $P(a,b)$ consists of permutations of $[N]$ whose strict excedance-position set is exactly $[b-1]$, allowing fixed points. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nHuman follow-ups · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/human-follow-ups)\\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; ## An Improved Lower Bound for the Complex Grothendieck Constant ... 7 Sept 2026 · arXiv · paper ... ## Kalai's Conjecture for Tight Trees\\n  * Small undecidable groups and fixed-size Adian–Rabin families\\n  * Small undecidable groups and fixed-size Adian–Rabin families\\n\\n## The bounds $\\\\hbar _{p,x}$ℏ p,x​ ≲ $\\\\beta _{p,x}^{2}$β p,x 2​ and $\\\\beta _{p,x}$β p,x​ ≲ $\\\\hbar _{p,x}^{2}$ℏ p,x 2​ are sharp\\n\\nEmiel Lorist, Jan van Neerven\\n\\n9 Sept 2026 · arXiv · Online · arXiv preprint\\n\\n  * The bounds $\\\\hbar _{p,x}$ℏ p,x​ ≲ $\\\\beta _{p,x}^{2}$β p,x 2​ and $\\\\beta _{p,x}$β p,x​ ≲ $\\\\hbar _{p,x}^{2}$ℏ p,x 2​ are sharp\\n\\n## A counterexample to Köthe's conjecture and a question of Rowen\\n\\nTom Adamczewski, Bernhard Böhmler, Rene Marczinzik\\n\\n7 Sept 2026 · arXiv · Online · arXiv preprint\\n\\n  * AI-found counterexample to Köthe’s matrix conjecture\\n\\n## An Improved Lower Bound for the Complex Grothendieck Constant\\n\\nShengtao Guo, Etha